## Scope: this is a reader, and that is deliberate

`vhlab-NewStim-matlab` will not be fully ported. Its presentation half is built
on Psychtoolbox, which exists only for MATLAB, so there is nothing to port it
to: 49 of its 417 `.m` files call `Screen()` directly and the lineage that
reaches them is larger still. This library reads the data structures NewStim
wrote -- principally the `stimscript` stored as `saveScript` in a `stims.mat`
-- and does not present stimuli, drive triggers, or talk to a stimulus
computer.

Reading is the limit in the other direction too. MATLAB stores `saveScript` as
an old-style `@stimscript` object, which `scipy.io.loadmat` surfaces as a
MATLAB-object wrapper rather than a struct and which `scipy.io.savemat` cannot
write at all. So a `stims.mat` cannot be round-tripped even in principle, and
the construction half of every class -- `append`, `insert`, `remove`, `set`,
`setparameters`, the `'graphical'` branch of each constructor -- is out of
scope for that reason as much as for the Psychtoolbox one.

Two practical consequences for anyone writing the first reader:

- Test fixtures have to be real `stims.mat` files recorded from MATLAB and
  committed to this repository. They cannot be synthesized in the test, because
  `savemat` cannot write the object the test needs to read.
- The MATLAB constructors call `NewStimListAdd` to register the class with the
  package's runtime registry (`NewStimProcs/`). Python needs no such registry;
  drop that call rather than translating it.

## Namespace

The package is `vhlab_newstim`, in a src-layout: importable code lives under
`src/vhlab_newstim/`. Functions and classes keep their MATLAB names, so a
MATLAB `getDisplayOrder` is a Python `getDisplayOrder`, not `get_display_order`
-- see `naming_policy` in the bridge files. Where MATLAB spells the same idea
two ways (`@stimscript/getDisplayOrder` and `@rcgratingstim/getdisplayorder`),
keep both spellings; they are different functions and unifying them silently
would lose that.

## Folder mapping

The Python package does not mirror the MATLAB folder names; the abbreviations
are decoded here and nowhere else, so keep this table current.

| MATLAB folder            | Python package                 |
|--------------------------|--------------------------------|
| `Scripts/`               | `src/vhlab_newstim/scripts/`   |
| `Stimuli/`               | `src/vhlab_newstim/stimuli/`   |
| `Stimuli/Display objs/`  | `src/vhlab_newstim/display/`   |

`NewStimUtilities/` is in the reading scope but has no Python subpackage yet;
it is accounted for at area level in the top-level bridge file. Give it a
subpackage and its own bridge file in the commit that ports the first function
out of it, and add the row above. Every other MATLAB folder is out of scope,
per area, in the top-level bridge file.

## Bridge files

Every `vhlab_newstim` package carries a `vhlab_newstim_matlab_python_bridge.yaml`
recording, for each function, where it came from in MATLAB and what was decided
along the way. This is the same convention NDI-python, did-python and
vhlab-library-python use (`ndi_matlab_python_bridge.yaml`,
`did_matlab_python_bridge.yaml`, `vhlib_matlab_python_bridge.yaml`); the file
name is `<python package>_matlab_python_bridge.yaml`.

- `src/vhlab_newstim/vhlab_newstim_matlab_python_bridge.yaml` -- the folder
  mapping above, the scope decision, the status vocabulary, a per-area decision
  for every part of vhlab-NewStim-matlab including the Psychtoolbox areas that
  will never be ported, and `downstream_requirements`: what is known to be
  waiting on this repository.
- `scripts/`, `stimuli/`, `display/` -- one file each, per function.

Each function entry carries:

- `matlab_path` / `python_path` -- the mapping, in both directions. Someone
  looking for the Python home of a MATLAB function, or asking whether it was
  ported at all, should get their answer from these files in one lookup.
  `python_path` is present only once something is ported.
- `matlab_class` -- the MATLAB class a method belongs to, since the same method
  name (`getparameters`, `duration`, `loadstim`) is defined by many classes and
  the name alone does not identify an entry. `matlab_path` is the unique key.
- `matlab_last_sync_hash` -- the vhlab-NewStim-matlab revision the entry was
  checked against, so drift is detectable rather than assumed.
- `input_arguments` / `output_arguments` -- with `type_matlab` and
  `type_python`, and `python_name` wherever an argument had to be renamed.
  Required once a function is `ported`.
- `decision_log` -- where a port deliberately diverges, and why. This is the
  field that stops the same question being re-litigated.
- `status` -- one of `ported`, `not_yet_ported`, `not_applicable`,
  `ported_elsewhere`, `deprecated_in_matlab`, `does_not_exist`, defined in
  `status_vocabulary` in the top-level bridge file. `partially_ported` is for
  areas only: a single function that diverges is `ported`, with the divergence
  in its `decision_log`.

`not_applicable` and `not_yet_ported` are never interchangeable here.
`not_applicable` means the Psychtoolbox-and-hardware decision above, or
construction, or a GUI; `not_yet_ported` means a function that is in the
reading scope and simply has no Python implementation yet. Keeping those two
apart is most of the value of these files.

A `ported` entry looks like this:

```yaml
  - name: getDisplayOrder
    matlab_class: stimscript
    type: method
    status: ported
    matlab_path: "Scripts/@stimscript/getDisplayOrder.m"
    matlab_last_sync_hash: "0ae6822"
    python_path: "src/vhlab_newstim/scripts/stimscript.py"
    input_arguments:
      - name: S
        type_matlab: "stimscript object"
        type_python: "StimScript"
        python_name: "self"
    output_arguments:
      - name: dispOrder
        type_matlab: "double array"
        type_python: "np.ndarray"
    decision_log: >
      Returns 1-based indices into the stimulus list, kept 1-based in Python
      under the indexing policy: they are a user-facing concept that also
      appears in stimtimes.txt.
```

When you port a function, update its entry in the same commit. When you decide
NOT to port something, say so with a `not_applicable` or `not_yet_ported` entry
and a reason -- otherwise "not ported" and "deliberately skipped" are
indistinguishable, and the next person re-does the research.

`tests/test_bridge.py` enforces the mechanical part: every module has an entry,
every `python_path` exists, every `status` is from the vocabulary, every entry
has a `decision_log`, and the per-area counts in the top-level file still sum to
the recorded total. What it cannot check -- because vhlab-NewStim-matlab is not
checked out in CI -- is that every MATLAB file still has an entry. When you bump
`matlab_last_sync_hash`, diff the MATLAB tree yourself and add entries for
anything new.

## Testing

Unit tests should be written for all functions and classes. This repository
uses `pytest` (`vhlab-library-python` uses `unittest`; do not import its
runner).

```bash
pip install -e ".[test]"
pytest -v
```

CI runs the suite on Python 3.10, 3.11 and 3.12, plus `ruff check src/ tests/`,
on every push and pull request.
